% CLAIM: Marton reinterprets the deep approach to learning in terms of
%   variation theory: the deep-approach learner creates the necessary patterns
%   of variation and invariance for themselves.
% FOUND-VIA: full-text search of the chapter PDFs in
%   ~/devel/edu/research/necessary-conditions-of-learning/modules
%   (pdftotext NCOL-ch5.pdf | grep -i "deep approach"), 2026-07-02.
% PICKED: Ch. 5 "The Art of Learning", sections "Reinterpreting Approaches to
%   Learning" through "Approaches to Learning, and Variation" (pp. 142--151).
% QUOTE (p. 142): "Those adopting a deep approach tried to find the main point
%   in the text, and the words for capturing it, by trying alternatives."
% QUOTE (p. 142): "The distinction was based on the learners' own accounts of
%   how they went about learning (i.e., how they experienced their own
%   attempts to learn)."
% QUOTE (p. 145): "Towards such an end, the learner has to create the
%   necessary patterns of variation and invariance. This is the deep approach
%   to learning in terms of the theory of variation."
% QUOTE (p. 151): "First, it was argued that the more powerful, deep approach
%   to learning is more varied. Second, it was argued that the less powerful,
%   surface approach to learning is less varied."
% --- contrast-before-induction claims (Ch. 3 "Sameness and Difference in
%     Learning", pp. 45--47; found via pdftotext NCOL-full.pdf | grep -n
%     induction, 2026-07-03):
% QUOTE (p. 46): "the arrangement does not work and cannot work, for the
%   simple reason that if you cannot see the greenness of any of three green,
%   though otherwise different, things, you cannot see what they have in
%   common, either."
% QUOTE (p. 46): "as a result of the simultaneous experience of things that
%   differ in color but are the same otherwise"
% QUOTE (p. 47): "We might imagine two teachers who choose to juxtapose two
%   different sets of three objects [...] One is betting on induction, the
%   other on contrast. The latter wins."
% QUOTE (p. 89): "If we want to find out to what extent they have learned to
%   do so, we should not point out those aspects for them but let the
%   students discern them by themselves."  (Ch. 4, on assessment; grounds
